# r_ion_Shannon.dat -- effective ionic radii and crystal radii, Shannon (1976) Table 1.
#
# CITE:  Shannon, R. D. (1976). Revised effective ionic radii and systematic
#        studies of interatomic distances in halides and chalcogenides.
#        Acta Cryst. A32, 751-767. doi:10.1107/S0567739476001551
#        Table 1, pp. 752-753.
#
# STATUS: every number below is Shannon's, transcribed for this package from
# the printed page; no file of anyone else's was copied. The two digital
# copies named under CHECKS were read as checks only and supplied no value.
# Cite the article, never this file or a copy, as the source of a radius.
#
# TRANSCRIPTION (2026-10-02). Read row by row from 400 dpi images of the two
# printed pages: 497 rows, 83 + 83 + 83 on p. 752 and 83 + 83 + 82 on p. 753
# (`block` is page.column, `row` the row within that column block).
#
# CHECKS, with counts. No check changed a transcribed value.
#   1. The PDF's own publisher OCR layer: agrees exactly on 340 rows; the other
#      157 (60 of them a 4 -> 6 digit swap) were re-read at 500 dpi, and the
#      page confirmed this transcription in all 157.
#   2. The Imperial College Shannon database (abulafia.mt.ic.ac.uk/shannon,
#      read 2026-10-02): 497 rows; CR, IR and CN identical on all 497. One
#      flag differs: Cr2+ VI HS is R* there and R on the page.
#   3. Wikipedia, "Ionic radius" (read 2026-10-02): 419 of 430 cells agree.
#      Two are wrong there: Pa3+ VI CR is 1.16 there and 1.18 on the page;
#      Gd3+ VI IR is 0.935 there and 0.938 on the page. The other 9 cells are
#      not Table 1 values (other sources, or ions with no Table 1 row).
#   4. Shannon's own relation, "crystal radii differ from traditional radii
#      only by a constant factor of 0.14 A" (pp. 759-760): CR = IR + 0.14 for
#      every cation and IR - 0.14 for every anion, on 496 of 497 rows. The
#      exception is N5+ III, printed .044 / -.104 (0.148), as the page has it.
#
# COLUMNS (Shannon's definitions, p. 752 caption and pp. 759-760):
#   ion     element symbol; OH is the hydroxide ion, D deuterium
#   charge  formal oxidation state as printed (CO+2 -> 2, O-2 -> -2)
#   ec      electronic configuration, exactly as printed; - for OH-, which prints none
#   cn      coordination number as a Roman numeral; suffix SQ = square
#           planar, PY = pyramidal
#   spin    HS or LS as printed, - where the table prints none
#   cr_A    crystal radius (A), on r(VI O2-) = 1.26 A and r(VI F-) = 1.19 A,
#           the Fumi & Tosi (1964) definition; Shannon: they "correspond more
#           closely to the physical size of ions in a solid" (p. 760)
#   ir_A    effective ionic radius (A), the traditional scale on
#           r(VI O2-) = 1.40 A; Shannon keeps it "for comparison of unit-cell
#           volumes and interatomic distances" (p. 760)
#   flags   as printed, - where none (p. 760):
#             R  from r^3 vs V plots
#             C  calculated from bond-length--bond-strength equations
#             E  estimated ("implies poor or nonexistent structural data")
#             *  most reliable: >= 5 structures agree within +/-0.01 A
#             M  from a compound with metallic conductivity ("may be too small
#                for use in compounds having localized electrons")
#             A  Ahrens (1952) ionic radius;  P  Pauling (1960) crystal radius
#             ?  doubtful -- defined, but printed on no row
#   page block row   where the row is printed
#
# AS PRINTED, deliberately: radii carry the printed number of decimals with a
# leading zero added (.675 -> 0.675); No2+ VI IR is printed 1.1 and kept so.
# Five radii are printed negative (H+ I and II, D+ II, C4+ III, N5+ III).
# Sb3+ IR(V) 0.80 > IR(VI) 0.76 is printed so. Four EC entries are not the
# physical configuration (Ce3+ 6S1, La3+ 4D10, Cf3+ 6D1, Pa4+ 6D1); no radius
# is affected. The table has no N3- CN VI row and no row for H-.
#
# SCOPE (Shannon, pp. 751-753): fitted mostly to oxides and fluorides, and
# "more applicable to oxides than fluorides"; additivity fails with covalence,
# metallic character, distortion and partial occupancy. Ionic radii cannot
# describe a covalent contact (B-B, C-C): see r_cov_Pyykko.dat.
#
# columns: ion  charge  ec  cn  spin  cr_A  ir_A  flags  page  block  row

